    130          /**
    131           *  @brief      PI Regulator with clamping anti-windup
    132           *  @details    PI controller algorithm.
    133           *              Definitions:
    134           *
    135           *              <b>e(t)</b> - input error in continuous time domain.
    136           *
    137           *              <b>u(t)</b> - controller output in continuous time domain.
    138           *
    139           *              <b>e(k)</b> - input error at step k - discrete time domain.
    140           *
    141           *              <b>u(k)</b> - controller output at step k - discrete time domain.
    142           *
    143           *              <b>e_max</b> - max range of e(k).
    144           *
    145           *              <b>u_max</b> - max range of u(k).
    146           *
    147           *            PI controller algorithm in <b>continuous time domain</b>:
    148           *
    149           *          \f[ u(t) = K\left(e(t) +\frac{1}{T_i}\int_0^t \! e(t) \, \mathrm{d}t. \right)\f]
    150           *           where:
    151           *            K :  controller gain
    152           *
    153           *            Ti: integral time constant
    154           *
    155           *           PI controller algorithm in <b>fractional arithmetic</b>:
    156           *
    157           *           \f[
    158           *             \begin{aligned}
    159           *                & u_f(k) = K_{p_{sc}} \cdot e_f(k) + u_f(k - 1) + K_{i_{sc}} \cdot e_f(k) \\
    160           *                & e_f(k) = \frac{e(k)}{e_{max}}\\
    161           *                & u_f(k) = \frac{u(k)}{u_{max}}\\
    162           *                & K_{p_{sc}} =  K \frac{e(k)}{e_{max}}\\
    163           *                & K_{i_{sc}} =  K \frac{T}{T_i}\frac{e(k)}{e_{max}}\\
    164           *             \end{aligned}
    165           *           \f]
    166           *           where:
    167           *           <b>T</b> : sampling time
    168           *
    169           *  The <b>anti-windup</b> technique is implemented as follows.
    170           *
    171           *  It stops integration when the sum of proportional and integral components exceeds the output limits
    172           *  and the two components have the same sign.
    173           *
    174           *  @param[in]      pi_params       = PI parameters: gains and limits
    175           *  @param[in]      err             = PI input error in Q17.15 notation
    176           *  @param[in, out] integ_term_k_1  = PI state variable in Q2.30 notation
    177           *  @return         PI output after saturation in Q17.15 notation
    178           */

